# `edition = "2024"` needs rust 1.85, and - the part that matters here - it implies
# `resolver = "3"`. With no `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml` and no `Cargo.lock` in the
# source tree, that makes cargo resolve the newest dependencies the toolchain it finds
# can actually build, per distribution and with no pinning on our side: a 1.85 toolchain
# gets dependency versions that still declare 1.85, a current one gets the latest. Only
# crates that declare a `rust-version` can be placed, and an incompatible version is
# still chosen when nothing else matches the requirement (`fallback`, not a rule), so
# this is correct rather than exact. Committing a lockfile would make it exact - at the
# price of pinning every distribution to the oldest toolchain we support.
# Do not "simplify" this to `edition = "2021"`: that silently selects `resolver = "2"`,
# which is not rust-version aware at all, and every distribution would then resolve the
# very newest dependencies regardless of its toolchain.
#
# Three spellings of the toolchain, because the older releases keep a modern rustc under
# a different package name while their default `rustc`/`cargo` are far too old:
#   trixie, sid       `rustc`/`cargo` are 1.85 / 1.95 - the plain names are right
#   bookworm          `rustc` is 1.63, but `rustc-web`/`cargo-web` (in main, so that
#                     Firefox can be built) are 1.85, and `rustc-web` Provides
#                     `rustc (= 1.85...)`. `cargo-web` must be preferred over `cargo`:
#                     bookworm's `cargo` is 0.66 and only Depends on `rustc (>= 1.24)`,
#                     so apt would happily pair it with rustc-web - and cargo is what
#                     parses the manifest, so it rejects edition 2024 on its own.
#   jammy, noble      `rustc`/`cargo` are 1.75; `rustc-1.85`/`cargo-1.85` are in
#                     <release>-updates/universe. They install into
#                     /usr/lib/rust-1.85/bin rather than /usr/bin and Provide nothing,
#                     which is what `debian/rules` puts on $PATH.
# `cargo` is left unversioned on purpose: Debian numbered it 0.66 in bookworm and only
# lined it up with rustc from trixie onwards, so `cargo (>= 1.85)` would be unsatisfiable
# on exactly the release that needs the constraint.
              ,cargo-web | cargo-1.85 | cargo
              ,rustc-web | rustc-1.85 | rustc (>= 1.85)
# `spng-sys` and `libz-sys` compile C sources with the `cc` crate:
              ,gcc
              ,zlib1g-dev
# `native-tls` (`ssl://` and `wss://`):
              ,libssl-dev
# `libwebp-sys`, via the `webp-dylib` feature - see debian/rules:
              ,libwebp-dev
# `turbojpeg-sys`; cmake and nasm are for the vendored fallback - see debian/rules:
              ,libturbojpeg0-dev
              ,cmake
              ,nasm [amd64 i386]
